# The rauc-update producer: the `mos-rauc-update` package, out of the rauc-sign
# crate. Read by build-env/deb/producers.sh and build-env/deb/build.sh; the
# convention is documented in build-env/deb/README.md.
#
# ONE PACKAGE, TWO BINARIES. `rauc-verify` is the metadata and target verifier
# and `rauc-update` is the transport and policy layer over the same walk; they
# are two halves of one device-side client, built from one commit and speaking
# one on-disk state file, so splitting them into two packages would only create
# a pair that must always be installed together at one version.
#
# `rauc-sign` -- the third binary the crate declares -- is deliberately NOT
# here. It is the release-side signing tool: it loads private keys and runs on a
# build host, never on a device. pkgs/rauc-sign/hack/build-deb.sh asserts its
# absence rather than merely not naming it.
PACKAGES="mos-rauc-update"
ARCHES="amd64 arm64"

# No unit, so no enablement link. The zero is WRITTEN rather than omitted
# because a package that starts nothing and a package nobody counted are
# indistinguishable in a field that is left out. Nothing on a device schedules
# this client yet -- what would start it is mosd's orchestration, which is a
# separate workstream -- and a package that shipped a timer would be this
# directory deciding an update cadence nobody asked it to.
ENABLEMENT="mos-rauc-update=0"

# The cargo cross-compile and the producer-independence assertion. Neither is a
# docker build, so neither can be a key above.
PREPARE="prepare.sh"

# No VERSION_FROM. This is a FIRST-PARTY package -- the code is in this
# repository, not an upstream tarball repacked -- so it carries the workspace
# version in front of the pool's `+git<commit><dirty>-1` stamp, exactly as the
# mosd producers do. build-env/deb/README.md states that rule.

# `family` is pkgs/rauc-sign/deb/, which holds the copyright file. It is not
# inside this producer's directory, which is the build context.
BUILD_CONTEXTS="family=pkgs/rauc-sign/deb"
